C-70..C-72: an adversarial pass over the text-to-decision parsers

Five defects had surfaced in core/shell_policy.py and core/bio_policy.py in a
single day, four of them fail-open, and every one lived at the same seam: where
text becomes a decision. A .bio comment, an empty stdin, a redirection, a
newline. Five accidents in one place is not luck, so this pass went looking
instead of waiting. It found six more, five of them fail-open. All were
reproduced before being fixed.

C-70 / O-27 — separators
shlex groups a run of punctuation into ONE token, and membership was tested
against a fixed list of spellings. The pipe-with-stderr operator and the
case-fallthrough operator were not in it, so a `curl` piped that way into
`bash` collapsed into a single sub-command named `curl`: the `bash` was never
seen as a program and the interpreter hardening — which exists for exactly this
case — never ran. Membership is now tested per CHARACTER.

Backticks were the same defect in different clothes. `$( … )` split only
because parentheses happen to be separators; `` echo `rm -rf /` `` did not
split at all. Backticks are now expanded in the quote-aware pass that handles
newlines, including inside double quotes, where the string is closed around the
substitution and reopened after it so the command comes into the open without
unbalancing the quoting.

C-71 / O-26 — program identity
One cause, a fail-open and an over-block. Windows resolves commands
case-insensitively and its executables carry `.exe`, but the comparison was a
plain string match on basename. In denylist-only mode `RM -RF /` RAN under a
denylist forbidding `rm -rf`; and a virtualenv interpreter given by path was
REFUSED although `python` was allowlisted. Comparison is now OS-aware.
Deliberately narrow: only `.exe` is stripped — a .bat or .cmd is a script, not
the same artefact as the bare name — and case is folded only on Windows, since
off Windows `RM` genuinely is not `rm`.

C-72 / O-28 — written denials
Two ways a DENY could be silently absent. The parser accepted only double
quotes, so `DENY 'git push'` was dropped with no error and no enforcement. And
matching was an exact token prefix, so `git -C /tmp/repo push` walked past
`git push` by putting an option in between. Both quoting styles are read now,
in ALLOW too, and the denied words must appear in order among the arguments.

Accepted over-block, stated rather than discovered later: `git commit -m push`
now matches, because after quote removal the message is the token `push`.
Refusing too much is loud; refusing too little is what O-25 was. The scope
limit is unchanged: a denylist is defence-in-depth (C-63), `rm -r -f /` still
evades an entry written `rm -rf`, and the boundary remains the filesystem and
network scope. What is fixed is narrower and more important — a rule the author
WROTE must not be silently missing.

The recurring cure is one idea: where a fixed list decides, whatever the list
omits fails open, so decide by property instead. Separators became a character
test rather than a set of spellings, exactly as the anchor denies became a
namespace with carve-outs (I-52) and the hermetic baseline became a prefix
rather than named variables.

O-29 left OPEN on purpose: shell keywords are still read as program names, so
`for … do … done` is refused for invoking a program called `for`. The repair is
grammar, not a lookup — `if`/`then`/`do`/`while` are followed by a command that
must still be checked, `for`/`case`/`in` by a word list that is not an
invocation — and getting that backwards would hide a command behind a keyword.
A quick table at the end of a long session is how the next O-25 gets written.

Suite: 51 proofs, 50 pass + 1 skip. Ledger: 51 proven, 6 blocked, 4 planned of
72; 14 open obstacles of 29. Decision I-55.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
